Scenario-Based DevOps Interview Questions (Real Production)

> Production Outage

❓ Your production website is down. What do you do?

Best-practice answer flow:
	1.	Check monitoring (Grafana / CloudWatch)
	2.	Check last deployment (Jenkins / ArgoCD)
	3.	Roll back if needed
	4.	Check logs (Pod / App / Nginx)
	5.	Scale or restart services
	6.	Identify root cause
	7.	Write post-mortem

❌ Don’t say “restart server” — say rollback + RCA


> Kubernetes Pods Crashing

❓ Pods are in CrashLoopBackOff. How do you debug?

Expected steps:

kubectl describe pod
kubectl logs <pod>
kubectl get events
kubectl exec -it <pod> -- sh

Check:
	•	Environment variables
	•	DB connectivity
	•	Missing secrets
	•	Application errors


> Terraform Broke Production

❓ A Terraform apply deleted a live resource. What went wrong?

You should say:
	•	No approval process
	•	No plan review
	•	No remote state lock
	•	No environment separation

Correct design:
	•	GitOps
	•	terraform plan → review → apply
	•	Separate state per env
	•	S3 + DynamoDB lock

> Zero-Downtime Deployment

❓ How do you deploy without users noticing?

Expected:
	•	Kubernetes rolling update
	•	Readiness probes
	•	Service load balancer
	•	Canary or Blue-Green
	•	Automatic rollback

> Database Change Caused Failure

❓ New version broke DB queries. What do you do?

Correct answer:
	•	Rollback app
	•	Restore DB if needed
	•	Add backward-compatible schema
	•	Use migrations
	•	Deploy in canary mode next time

> CI/CD Pipeline is Too Slow

❓ Build takes 40 minutes. How do you optimize?

They want:
	•	Parallel stages
	•	Cache dependencies
	•	Use Docker layer cache
	•	Remove unused tests
	•	Use smaller base images

Secrets Exposed in Git

❓ Someone committed AWS keys. What do you do?

Expected:
	1.	Revoke keys immediately
	2.	Rotate secrets
	3.	Remove from Git history
	4.	Use Secrets Manager / Vault
	5.	Add pre-commit hooks

> One Pod Gets All Traffic

❓ Only one Pod is overloaded.

You should say:
	•	Service load balancing
	•	Readiness probe misconfigured
	•	Session affinity enabled
	•	Fix service or ingress


> Kubernetes Node Crashed

❓ One node died. What happens?

Correct answer:
	•	Pods rescheduled
	•	Load balancer redirects
	•	Auto Scaling adds new node
	•	Zero impact if designed correctly

> Release Caused High Latency

❓ After deployment, response time increased.

Expected:
	•	Compare old vs new version
	•	Check CPU, memory, DB, API
	•	Rollback
	•	Find slow query or bug
	•	Fix before redeploy

> How to Answer Like a Senior DevOps Engineer

Always show:
	1.	Monitoring
	2.	Automation
	3.	Rollback
	4.	Root cause
	5.	Prevention
